module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularHilbertCoefficientDatum

/-! # Scalar extension of supported formal division

Source: Bierstone--Milman, Invent. Math. 128 (1997), Lemma 7.3, Corollary 7.4,
and the field-extension argument in the proof of Theorem 9.4.
(blueprint: lem-trf-formal-division-scalar-extension).
-/

@[expose] public section
set_option autoImplicit false
noncomputable section
universe u v

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertCoefficient

/-- Supported homogeneous division and supported ideal expansions extend to arbitrary
coefficient fields. No Jacobian conditions are needed.

Source: BM 1997, Lemma 7.3 and Corollary 7.4, p.263; proof of Theorem 9.4, p.278; Remark 3.8, p.234.
(blueprint: lem-trf-formal-division-scalar-extension).

Proof: Fix a degree prefix and a homogeneous degree. The quotient and remainder
supports index finite monomial bases, so the homogeneous division map is an invertible
finite matrix. Extend that matrix and its inverse along the field embedding. Their
inverse identities give unique homogeneous division over the new field, for every
prefix. This argument permits repeated vertices as well: the assumed uniqueness itself
excludes any conflicting supported columns. Starting with the least homogeneous part
of an arbitrary series, divide, subtract the quotients times the full generators, and
repeat in increasing total degree. The orders of the generators ensure that every
coefficient stabilizes. This constructs continuous supported formal division over the
extension field and proves uniqueness by the first nonzero homogeneous degree.
The source supported expansions show that the source ideal is generated by the given
finite family. Its extended ideal is therefore generated by their images. The remainder
of every polynomial multiple of an image generator vanishes: map the corresponding
source division, then use uniqueness. Truncating arbitrary multipliers and using
continuity proves the same for formal multiples and their finite sums. Thus every
member of the extended ideal has a supported expansion. Empty families give the zero
ideal, and degree-zero generators cause no exceptional case in the finite matrices. -/
theorem formal_division_scalar_extension
    {L : Type u} [Field L] {E : Type v} [Field E] (ι : L →+* E)
    {m n s : ℕ} (α : Fin s → Fin n →₀ ℕ)
    (K : Ideal (MvPowerSeries (Fin m ⊕ Fin n) L))
    (f : Fin s → MvPowerSeries (Fin m ⊕ Fin n) L)
    (hmem : ∀ i : Fin s, f i ∈ K)
    (horder : ∀ i : Fin s, (f i).order = ((α i).degree : ℕ∞))
    (hdivision : homogeneousDivision α f)
    (hgen : ∀ h ∈ K, ∃ q : Fin s → MvPowerSeries (Fin m ⊕ Fin n) L,
      h = ∑ i : Fin s, q i * f i ∧
        ∀ i : Fin s, supported (q i) (inDelta (fun j ↦ fullVertex m (α j)) i)) :
    homogeneousDivision α (fun i ↦ MvPowerSeries.map ι (f i)) ∧
    ∀ h ∈ K.map (MvPowerSeries.map ι),
      ∃ q : Fin s → MvPowerSeries (Fin m ⊕ Fin n) E,
        h = ∑ i : Fin s, q i * MvPowerSeries.map ι (f i) ∧
          ∀ i : Fin s, supported (q i) (inDelta (fun j ↦ fullVertex m (α j)) i) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertCoefficient
